{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md; sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md; nl -ba collab/1788950282826-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r5.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '400,500p'; git diff -- src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc test/dao/space/index_test.cljc docs/design/dao.space.index.md; nl -ba collab/1788950986527-storage-space-transactor-phase1.glm-5.3.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md; sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md; nl -ba collab/1788950282826-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r5.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '400,500p'; git diff -- src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc test/dao/space/index_test.cljc docs/design/dao.space.index.md; nl -ba collab/1788950986527-storage-space-transactor-phase1.glm-5.3.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: code-review-pr\ndescription: >-\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\n---\n\n# Code Review PR Skill\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\n\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\n\n# 2. Review last commit\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\n\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\n\n# 4. Save review report to file\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## Code Review Workflow\n\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\n\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\n\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\n\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\n\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\n\n```markdown\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\n\n## Executive Summary\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\n\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\n  ```diff\n  - problematic code\n  + suggested fix\n  ```\n\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\n\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\n\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Utility Script Reference\n\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\n\n---\n\n## Common Review Pitfalls\n\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n   400\t\n   401\t### D9 — the local-log wiring requirement\n   402\t\n   403\t> The host composition supplies `dao.space` a local handle created by\n   404\t> `dao.stream.memory-log/create!`. Its declared complete retention makes\n   405\t> fresh `:oldest` cursors true origin cursors for `derive-next-t` and\n   406\t> `publish-index!`; supplying an evicting transport is a host-assembly defect.\n   407\t\n   408\tA **declaration, not a check** (D2, D6). §8 states where it lives, what each\n   409\tplacement must say, and — per F3 — the honest form of the residual risk.\n   410\t\n   411\t---\n   412\t\n   413\t## 2. The invariants this plan is accountable to\n   414\t\n   415\t### Transactor\n   416\t\n   417\t| # | Invariant | |\n   418\t|---|---|---|\n   419\t| T1 | Every `append!`/`transact!` writes exactly ONE atomic transaction record through exactly one local append, so no reader observes a torn transaction | `[D]` |\n   420\t| T2 | `t` comes from a per-wrapper watermark, derived on open as 0 for empty history else 1 + max datom `t` | `[D]` |\n   421\t| T3 | A caller-supplied `:next-t` is rejected | `[D]` |\n   422\t| T4a | A malformed retained history fails the open | `[D]` |\n   423\t| T4b | A retention gap fails the open | **`[D✗]`** — the transport excludes `gap`. Replaced by T18; its fixture goes with it (§7). |\n   424\t| T5 | The watermark advances **iff** the local append succeeded; a failed or thrown append leaves the same `t` retryable | `[D]` + `[T]` :499, :515 |\n   425\t| T6 | Single-writer: two wrappers over one local stream derive the same `t` and write colliding records; a documented hazard | `[D]` + `[T]` :655 |\n   426\t| T7 | Close is per handle: it rejects further writes and neither closes nor erases the local stream or the intake pool | `[D]` + `[T]` :584 |\n   427\t| T8 | Close linearizes after an in-flight append | `[T]` :616 → `[T→D]` |\n   428\t| T9 | Opening creates, registers, or closes nothing | `[T]` :273 → `[T→D]` |\n   429\t| T10 | Entity maps require `:db/id`; an explicit datom `t` is rejected; `[e a v]`/`[e a v nil m]` pad and validate | `[D]` |\n   430\t| T11 | `publish!` passes the local stream, pool and opts to `index/publish-index!` and returns its result | `[D]` |\n   431\t| T12 | The wrapper delegates reads | **`[T✗]`** |\n   432\t| T13 | `close!` returns `{:woke []}` | **`[T✗]`** for the transactor; `SchemaWrapper.close!` keeps it (D10) |\n   433\t| T14 | `closed?` is a public predicate | **`[T✗]`** — v2 *Explicitly Absent* |\n   434\t| T15 | An append receipt carries ok, the allocated `t`, and the datoms | `[T]` → preserved in v2 vocabulary **on the transactor**; schema re-wraps (D10) |\n   435\t| T16 | Intake pool members are validated with v2 `stream/writer?`; a non-empty collection is required | `[D]` |\n   436\t| T17 | A non-ok local append throws | **`[T✗]`** — returned as data (D3) |\n   437\t| T18 | The local stream is on a transport declaring complete retention. Supplying an evicting one is a host-assembly defect that is **detectable but deliberately not checked** (§8) | **`[D]`, new** |\n   438\t| T19 | Schema installs its next state only when the transactor answered ok | **`[D]`, new** |\n   439\t| T20 | Schema's v1 public results keep their v1 shape until schema's own plan | **`[D]`, new** — D10 |\n   440\t\n   441\t### Index\n   442\t\n   443\t| # | Invariant | |\n   444\t|---|---|---|\n   445\t| S1 | The read begins at the logical sequence's origin, because the transport declares complete retention | `[D]` |\n   446\t| S2 | An element is a canonical d5 vector or a transaction record | `[D]` |\n   447\t| S3 | The transaction record's exact shape | `[T→D]` |\n   448\t| S4 | `blocked`/`end` finish the read at the tail | `[D]` |\n   449\t| S5 | A gap throws before any emission | **`[D✗]`** — the transport excludes `gap` (§7) |\n   450\t| S6 | A malformed element, or a malformed **result shape**, throws before any emission | `[D]`, widened by D6's `checked` |\n   451\t| S7 | The two distinct element diagnostics | `[T]` |\n   452\t| S8 | Each element is validated and flattened as it is read, so the first defect in stream order is the one reported and no read happens past it | **`[T→D]`, kept** |\n   453\t| S9 | The local stream's transport declares complete retention | **`[D]`, new** — the same declaration as T18, stated on index because `publish-index!` depends on it independently of the transactor |\n   454\t| S10 | A well-formed outcome outside `ok`/`blocked`/`end` is a violated precondition and throws | **`[D]`, new** — the totality branch, now tested (F4) |\n   455\t\n   456\t**Published** — P1 the coordinate's exact shape `[D]`; P2 plain EDN, survives\n   457\ta stream round-trip `[T→D]`; P3 opening fetches exactly one blob, zero nodes\n   458\tfaulted `[D]`; P4 rows deferred, EAVT order, equal to `read-datoms` `[D]`; P5\n   459\ta store opened during a failed open is closed before the error propagates\n   460\t`[D]` — **no covering test today**, fixed in Phase 3; P6 the v1 adapter's\n   461\tprotocol vocabulary `[T]` — dies with the adapter; P7 two independent cursors\n   462\t`[T✗]`; P8 `covered-indexes` is a structural check `[D]`.\n   463\t\n   464\t---\n   465\t\n   466\t## 3. Phase 1 — expose the payload vocabulary\n   467\t\n   468\t`src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc`, its test, and one documentation line. **This\n   469\tphase is behaviour-neutral, and r4 made that true rather than claiming it.**\n   470\t\n   471\t1. Rename private `stream-payload-datoms` → private `element-datoms`. Body\n   472\t   unchanged; S3, S6, S7 preserved verbatim including both error messages.\n   473\t2. Add public `datoms-from-elements`: `(into [] (mapcat element-datoms)\n   474\t   elements)` — the vocabulary's public, seq-level spelling.\n   475\t3. **`snapshot-datoms` is not restructured.** It keeps calling\n   476\t   `element-datoms` incrementally inside its existing v1 read loop, so failure\n   477\t   ordering is untouched and S8 holds. Phase 2's v2 loop does the same (D6).\n   478\t4. Document `datoms-from-elements` in `dao.space.index.md`'s *Public surface*\n   479\t   **in this phase** — a public function and its documentation land together.\n   480\t   Note there, so no later reader \"cleans it up\": it has no `src` caller by\n   481\t   design. It is the vocabulary's public spelling; `snapshot-datoms` uses the\n   482\t   per-element form to preserve S8, and both go through `element-datoms`.\n   483\t\n   484\t**Proof.** Add `datoms-from-elements-is-the-local-stream-vocabulary` to\n   485\t`index_test`, pinning S2, S3, S6, S7 directly rather than only through\n   486\t`publish-index!`. Every existing test stays unchanged — which is the phase's\n   487\town neutrality check.\n   488\t\n   489\t## 4. Phase 2 — the swap\n   490\t\n   491\tOne phase: the `defopen` and `DaoStreamLog` cannot be half-deleted, and every\n   492\tconsumer breaks at the same instant.\n   493\t\n   494\t### 4.1 `src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc`\n   495\t\n   496\tDelete `DaoStreamLog`, `ds/defopen :transactor`, the `[dao.stream :as ds]`\n   497\trequire and the `#?(:cljs (:require-macros [dao.stream]))`.\n   498\t\n   499\t- `create!` per D2: validate the spec (reject `:next-t`; require a\n   500\t  `stream/reader?`-and-`stream/writer?` local stream; require a non-empty pool\ndiff --git a/docs/design/dao.space.index.md b/docs/design/dao.space.index.md\nindex e3541da..b979029 100644\n--- a/docs/design/dao.space.index.md\n+++ b/docs/design/dao.space.index.md\n@@ -136,6 +136,9 @@ trees therefore report the same O(1) count they actually contain.\n ;; the snapshot (reads and validates a local stream)\n (index/snapshot-datoms local-stream)              ; => flattened datom seq\n \n+;; the payload vocabulary's public, seq-level spelling\n+(index/datoms-from-elements elements)             ; => flattened datom vector\n+\n ;; the format's readers (every platform)\n (index/published-index {:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/file :path path}\n                        manifest-address)                  ; bounded d5 descriptor\n@@ -153,6 +156,18 @@ trees therefore report the same O(1) count they actually contain.\n (index/datom-t d) (index/datom-m d)\n ```\n \n+**`datoms-from-elements` is the payload vocabulary's public spelling.** A\n+local-stream element is one canonical d5 datom vector or one atomic\n+`{:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms [...]}}` record; `datoms-from-elements`\n+flattens a seq of them, validating each element through the same private\n+per-element rule (`element-datoms`) that rejects malformed datoms, malformed\n+transaction records, and non-elements with distinct diagnostics. It has no\n+`src` caller, by design — do not \"clean it up\": `snapshot-datoms` deliberately\n+uses the per-element form inside its read loop rather than this seq-level one,\n+so each element is validated and flattened as it is read (the first defect in\n+stream order is the one reported, and no read happens past it), and both\n+spellings go through the same `element-datoms`.\n+\n `publish-index!` semantics worth pinning:\n \n - **Builds into a temporary recording content handle.** The four indexes are\ndiff --git a/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc b/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc\nindex 9551390..15d805e 100644\n--- a/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc\n+++ b/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc\n@@ -408,7 +408,7 @@\n ;; publish-index! — build, record, append\n ;; =============================================================================\n \n-(defn- stream-payload-datoms\n+(defn- element-datoms\n   [payload]\n   (if (and (map? payload) (contains? payload :dao.space/transaction))\n     (let [tx (:dao.space/transaction payload)\n@@ -439,6 +439,17 @@\n           {:payload payload})))))\n \n \n+(defn datoms-from-elements\n+  \"Flatten a seq of local-stream elements to canonical local d5 datoms —\n+   the payload vocabulary's public, seq-level spelling. Each element is\n+   validated and flattened by the same per-element rule snapshot-datoms\n+   applies inside its read loop: a canonical datom vector passes through, an\n+   atomic {:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms [...]}} record flattens to\n+   its datoms, and anything malformed throws.\"\n+  [elements]\n+  (into [] (mapcat element-datoms) elements))\n+\n+\n (defn snapshot-datoms\n   \"Eagerly snapshot an agent-local stream by walking `{:position 0}` with\n    ds/next. A stream element is either one canonical datom vector or one\n@@ -455,7 +466,7 @@\n         (map? result)\n         (if (and (contains? result :ok) (contains? result :cursor))\n           (recur (:cursor result)\n-                 (into datoms (stream-payload-datoms (:ok result))))\n+                 (into datoms (element-datoms (:ok result))))\n           (throw\n             (ex-info\n               \"malformed stream result: a successful read must carry both :ok and :cursor\"\ndiff --git a/test/dao/space/index_test.cljc b/test/dao/space/index_test.cljc\nindex 64e0d06..2fa35b7 100644\n--- a/test/dao/space/index_test.cljc\n+++ b/test/dao/space/index_test.cljc\n@@ -223,6 +223,69 @@\n         (is (empty? (intake-values intake)))))))\n \n \n+(deftest datoms-from-elements-is-the-local-stream-vocabulary\n+  (testing\n+    \"an element is one canonical d5 datom vector or one atomic transaction\n+          record; the seq-level spelling flattens both, in stream order\"\n+    (let [d1 [1 :person/name \"Ada\" 0 1]\n+          d2 [1 :person/role :architect 7 1]\n+          d3 [2 :work/status :todo 7 1]]\n+      (is (= [] (index/datoms-from-elements [])))\n+      (is (= [d1] (index/datoms-from-elements [d1])))\n+      (is (= [d1 d2 d3]\n+             (index/datoms-from-elements\n+               [d1 {:dao.space/transaction {:t 7, :datoms [d2 d3]}}])))))\n+  (testing \"a transaction record is exactly {:t n :datoms [canonical d5]}\"\n+    (let [tx-datoms [[1 :person/name \"Ada\" 7 1] [1 :person/role :architect 7 1]]]\n+      (is (= tx-datoms\n+             (index/datoms-from-elements\n+               [{:dao.space/transaction {:t 7, :datoms tx-datoms}}]))))\n+    (doseq [packet [{:dao.space/transaction {:t 1, :datoms :not-a-vector}}\n+                    {:dao.space/transaction {:t 1, :datoms []}}\n+                    {:dao.space/transaction {:t 1,\n+                                             :datoms [[1 :test/a :v 2 1]]}}\n+                    {:dao.space/transaction\n+                     {:t 1, :datoms [[1 :test/a :v 1 1 :source/forbidden]]}}\n+                    {:dao.space/transaction {:t -1,\n+                                             :datoms [[1 :test/a :v -1 1]]}}\n+                    {:dao.space/transaction\n+                     {:t 1, :datoms [[1 :test/a :v 1 1]], :extra :key}}]]\n+      (is (thrown-with-msg? #?(:cljs js/Error\n+                               :cljd Object\n+                               :default Exception)\n+                            #\"malformed dao\\.space transaction record\"\n+            (index/datoms-from-elements [packet]))\n+          (str \"must reject \" (pr-str packet)))))\n+  (testing\n+    \"a malformed element throws its distinct diagnostic — a malformed datom\n+          is not a malformed transaction record, and a non-element is neither\"\n+    (doseq [bad-datom [[:entity :test/a :v 0 1] [-16 :test/a :v 0 1]\n+                       [1 :unqualified :v 0 1] [1 \"test/a\" :v 0 1]\n+                       [1 :test/a :v -1 1] [1 :test/a :v 0 :db/assert]]]\n+      (is (thrown-with-msg? #?(:cljs js/Error\n+                               :cljd Object\n+                               :default Exception)\n+                            #\"malformed local datom\"\n+            (index/datoms-from-elements [bad-datom]))\n+          (str \"must reject datom \" (pr-str bad-datom))))\n+    (doseq [bad [\"not-an-element\" [1 :test/a :v] {:not :a-transaction}]]\n+      (is (thrown-with-msg? #?(:cljs js/Error\n+                               :cljd Object\n+                               :default Exception)\n+                            #\"local stream payload must be\"\n+            (index/datoms-from-elements [bad]))\n+          (str \"must reject \" (pr-str bad)))))\n+  (testing\n+    \"the public spelling and the snapshot read agree on the same elements:\n+          both go through the same per-element rule\"\n+    (let [d1 [1 :person/name \"Ada\" 0 1]\n+          d2 [1 :person/role :architect 7 1]\n+          d3 [2 :work/status :todo 7 1]\n+          elements [d1 {:dao.space/transaction {:t 7, :datoms [d2 d3]}}]]\n+      (is (= (index/datoms-from-elements elements)\n+             (index/snapshot-datoms (open-local elements)))))))\n+\n+\n (deftest publish-index-address-is-content-derived-and-stream-invariant\n   (testing\n     \"the manifest address derives from the manifest alone: identical local\n     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-09 11:10 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-09 18:10 +0700 (Asia/Bangkok)\n     3\tCoding-Agent: glm\n     4\tSession-ID: db17b4be-bb49-4dce-a31e-583a854ade64\n     5\t\n     6\t# Phase 1 complete — expose the payload vocabulary\n     7\t\n     8\tBranch `dao.stream-redesign-v2` at `ba90b3a`, tree clean before the edit.\n     9\tPhase 1 of the r5 plan (§3) only. Three files touched, nothing staged, nothing\n    10\tcommitted.\n    11\t\n    12\t## What was done\n    13\t\n    14\t`src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc` (15 insertions, 2 deletions):\n    15\t\n    16\t1. Private `stream-payload-datoms` renamed to private `element-datoms`.\n    17\t   **Body unchanged** — S3, S6, S7 preserved verbatim, including all three\n    18\t   error messages (\"malformed dao.space transaction record\", \"malformed local\n    19\t   datom: …\", \"local stream payload must be a datom or dao.space transaction\n    20\t   record\"). `stream-payload-datoms` had exactly two references in the tree,\n    21\t   both in this file (verified by grep before editing).\n    22\t2. Public `datoms-from-elements` added between `element-datoms` and\n    23\t   `snapshot-datoms`: `(into [] (mapcat element-datoms) elements)` — the\n    24\t   plan's exact body — with a docstring naming it the vocabulary's public,\n    25\t   seq-level spelling.\n    26\t3. **`snapshot-datoms` was not restructured.** The only change inside it is\n    27\t   the callee rename: `(into datoms (element-datoms (:ok result)))` stays\n    28\t   inside the existing v1 read loop, called per element as each read returns.\n    29\t   S8 is kept and promoted: failure ordering untouched, no read past the\n    30\t   first defect, no drain-then-flatten. The `ds/next` docstring mention and\n    31\t   the `:daostream/gap` branch are Phase 2's to remove, not Phase 1's.\n    32\t\n    33\t`test/dao/space/index_test.cljc` (63 insertions, **0 deletions**):\n    34\t\n    35\t4. One new deftest, `datoms-from-elements-is-the-local-stream-vocabulary`,\n    36\t   pinning S2, S3, S6, S7 directly through `index/datoms-from-elements`\n    37\t   rather than only through `publish-index!`:\n    38\t   - S2: both element kinds (a canonical d5 vector; an atomic transaction\n    39\t     record) flatten in stream order; empty and single-element seqs.\n    40\t   - S3: the transaction record's exact shape — a good flatten, plus six\n    41\t     rejection sub-cases (`:datoms` non-vector; empty; datom `t` mismatch;\n    42\t     6-slot datom; negative `t`; extra key in the `tx` map).\n    43\t   - S6/S7: the distinct diagnostics — six malformed 5-vectors throw\n    44\t     `#\"malformed local datom\"`, six malformed transaction records throw\n    45\t     `#\"malformed dao\\.space transaction record\"`, and non-elements (string,\n    46\t     3-vector, non-transaction map) throw `#\"local stream payload must be\"`.\n    47\t   - A final block ties the two spellings together:\n    48\t     `(index/datoms-from-elements elements)` equals\n    49\t     `(index/snapshot-datoms (open-local elements))` — both go through\n    50\t     `element-datoms`.\n    51\t\n    52\t`docs/design/dao.space.index.md` (15 insertions):\n    53\t\n    54\t5. `datoms-from-elements` documented in *Public surface* — the code-block\n    55\t   entry plus the prose note the plan requires: it has no `src` caller **by\n    56\t   design**, so no later reader \"cleans it up\"; it is the vocabulary's public\n    57\t   spelling; `snapshot-datoms` deliberately uses the per-element form inside\n    58\t   its read loop so each element is validated and flattened as it is read,\n    59\t   and both spellings go through `element-datoms`.\n    60\t\n    61\t## No existing test was modified\n    62\t\n    63\t**Zero.** The `index_test.cljc` diff is purely additive (63 insertions,\n    64\t0 deletions; `git diff` inspected). Every existing deftest, assertion, and\n    65\tfixture in every test file is byte-identical to `ba90b3a`. The phase's\n    66\tacceptance criterion — every existing test unchanged and green — holds in all\n    67\tthree lanes.\n    68\t\n    69\t## Verification — exact commands and counts\n    70\t\n    71\t`bb` and bare `clojure -M:cljd` invocations were denied by this session's\n    72\tpermission settings, so each lane ran as its exact underlying command\n    73\t(taken from `bb.edn`), except the cljd lane which ran as the real bb task\n    74\tthrough `mise exec`:\n    75\t\n    76\t| lane | command | result |\n    77\t|---|---|---|\n    78\t| clj | `clojure -M:test` (= `bb test:clj`) | **Ran 1432 tests containing 165341 assertions. 0 failures, 0 errors.** `Testing dao.space.index-test` present |\n    79\t| cljs | `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile slice-peer test` (= `bb test:cljs`) | **Ran 1342 tests containing 34913 assertions. 0 failures, 1 error.** `Testing dao.space.index-test` present and green in the node output |\n    80\t| cljd | `mise exec -- bb test:cljd` (full task: rebuilt `build/slice-peer` and `build/yin-repl-peer`, then `clojure -M:cljd test`) | **All tests passed!** (+1295). All 32 index deftests — the new one included — present in the generated `test/cljd-out/dao/space/index-test_test.dart` (verified: 32 `test(` entries; all 32 names enumerated) |\n    81\t| demo | `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile demo` | **Build completed. (212 files, 12 compiled, 0 warnings, 3.04s)** |\n    82\t| kondo | `clj -M:kondo --lint src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc test/dao/space/index_test.cljc` | **0 errors, 3 warnings — all pre-existing**, see below |\n    83\t| format | `mise exec -- cljstyle fix src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc test/dao/space/index_test.cljc`, then `cljstyle check` on the same two | fix changed nothing (the edits were already conformant); check exits clean |\n    84\t\n    85\t### The one cljs error is pre-existing and unrelated\n    86\t\n    87\t`wasm-eval-emits-telemetry-test` in `test/yin/vm/telemetry_test.cljc` fails\n    88\twith `ReferenceError: wasm is not defined`, plus two compile warnings\n    89\t(`No such namespace: wasm`) — all at `telemetry_test.cljc:101-109`, a file\n    90\tthis phase does not touch (this phase's diff is the three files listed\n    91\tabove). Root cause is in the committed history: `78b5262` (\"test(telemetry):\n    92\tremove wasm test\", 2026-09-08, an ancestor of `ba90b3a`) removed the\n    93\t`#?(:cljs [yin.vm.wasm :as wasm])` require but left the `#?(:cljs (deftest\n    94\twasm-eval-emits-telemetry-test …))` body in place, so the breakage is baked\n    95\tinto the base commit. Evidence: `git show 78b5262` diff touches only the ns\n    96\tblock; `git show HEAD:test/yin/vm/telemetry_test.cljc` still contains the\n    97\tdeftest at line 101. No `dao.space` namespace is involved. Left as found —\n    98\tfixing it is outside this brief's write authority.\n    99\t\n   100\t### The three kondo warnings are pre-existing\n   101\t\n   102\t`index.cljc:347` (Missing protocol method: cursor) and `index.cljc:360`\n   103\t(Unresolved protocol method: close!) are `PublishedIndexStream`'s v1 protocol\n   104\timpl — deleted in Phase 3 per D7. `index_test.cljc:30` (Missing protocol\n   105\tmethod: cursor) is `MalformedResultStream` — migrated in Phase 2 per §4.4.\n   106\tAll three anchor to lines this phase's diff does not touch (the index.cljc\n   107\tedit starts at line 411; the test edit at line 226); the diff is purely\n   108\tadditive there, so the identical warnings exist at `ba90b3a`.\n   109\t\n   110\t## Notes for the Phase 2 implementer\n   111\t\n   112\t- The two prompt corrections are confirmed in passing, not propagated into\n   113\t  any edit: the `schema_test` figure is 22 assertion sites (not 20), and\n   114\t  `stigmergy_test.clj:32`'s bare `[dao.stream.ringbuffer]` require is\n   115\t  Phase 2/3 residue, untouched here.\n   116\t- `grep -rn \"stream-payload-datoms\"` now returns nothing; the rename is\n   117\t  total.\n   118\t- Nothing was staged or committed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc | sed -n '405,480p'; nl -ba test/dao/space/index_test.cljc | sed -n '150,300p'; rg -n \\\"exact shape|transaction record|dao.space/transaction\\\" docs/design/dao.space.index.md collab/1788950282826-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r5.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | head -100; git diff --check; rg -n \\\"stream-payload-datoms\\\" . --glob '\"'!collab/**'\"' --glob '\"'!target/**'\"' --glob '\"'!build/**'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc | sed -n '405,480p'; nl -ba test/dao/space/index_test.cljc | sed -n '150,300p'; rg -n \\\"exact shape|transaction record|dao.space/transaction\\\" docs/design/dao.space.index.md collab/1788950282826-architect-space-transactor-v2-plan-r5.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | head -100; git diff --check; rg -n \\\"stream-payload-datoms\\\" . --glob '\"'!collab/**'\"' --glob '\"'!target/**'\"' --glob '\"'!build/**'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"   405\t\n   406\t\n   407\t;; =============================================================================\n   408\t;; publish-index! — build, record, append\n   409\t;; =============================================================================\n   410\t\n   411\t(defn- element-datoms\n   412\t  [payload]\n   413\t  (if (and (map? payload) (contains? payload :dao.space/transaction))\n   414\t    (let [tx (:dao.space/transaction payload)\n   415\t          t (:t tx)\n   416\t          datoms (:datoms tx)]\n   417\t      (when-not (and (map? tx)\n   418\t                     (= #{:t :datoms} (set (keys tx)))\n   419\t                     (integer? t)\n   420\t                     (not (neg? t))\n   421\t                     (vector? datoms)\n   422\t                     (seq datoms)\n   423\t                     (every? datom/local-datom? datoms)\n   424\t                     (every? #(= t (datom-t %)) datoms))\n   425\t        (throw (ex-info \"malformed dao.space transaction record\"\n   426\t                        {:payload payload})))\n   427\t      datoms)\n   428\t    (cond\n   429\t      (datom/local-datom? payload) [payload]\n   430\t      (and (vector? payload) (= 5 (count payload)))\n   431\t      (throw\n   432\t        (ex-info\n   433\t          \"malformed local datom: e and t must be non-negative integers, m an integer, and a a namespaced keyword\"\n   434\t          {:datom payload}))\n   435\t      :else\n   436\t      (throw\n   437\t        (ex-info\n   438\t          \"local stream payload must be a datom or dao.space transaction record\"\n   439\t          {:payload payload})))))\n   440\t\n   441\t\n   442\t(defn datoms-from-elements\n   443\t  \"Flatten a seq of local-stream elements to canonical local d5 datoms —\n   444\t   the payload vocabulary's public, seq-level spelling. Each element is\n   445\t   validated and flattened by the same per-element rule snapshot-datoms\n   446\t   applies inside its read loop: a canonical datom vector passes through, an\n   447\t   atomic {:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms [...]}} record flattens to\n   448\t   its datoms, and anything malformed throws.\"\n   449\t  [elements]\n   450\t  (into [] (mapcat element-datoms) elements))\n   451\t\n   452\t\n   453\t(defn snapshot-datoms\n   454\t  \"Eagerly snapshot an agent-local stream by walking `{:position 0}` with\n   455\t   ds/next. A stream element is either one canonical datom vector or one\n   456\t   atomic `{:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms [...]}}` record; transaction\n   457\t   records are flattened into their datoms. :blocked and :end finish the\n   458\t   snapshot at the current tail; :daostream/gap and malformed stream results,\n   459\t   datoms, or transaction records throw. publish-index! calls this to\n   460\t   completion before appending anything to an intake stream.\"\n   461\t  [local-stream]\n   462\t  (loop [cursor {:position 0}\n   463\t         datoms []]\n   464\t    (let [result (ds/next local-stream cursor)]\n   465\t      (cond\n   466\t        (map? result)\n   467\t        (if (and (contains? result :ok) (contains? result :cursor))\n   468\t          (recur (:cursor result)\n   469\t                 (into datoms (element-datoms (:ok result))))\n   470\t          (throw\n   471\t            (ex-info\n   472\t              \"malformed stream result: a successful read must carry both :ok and :cursor\"\n   473\t              {:result result})))\n   474\t        (= result :blocked) datoms\n   475\t        (= result :end) datoms\n   476\t        (= result :daostream/gap)\n   477\t        (throw (ex-info \"stream snapshot gap: position evicted before read\"\n   478\t                        {:cursor cursor}))\n   479\t        :else (throw (ex-info \"malformed stream signal\" {:signal result}))))))\n   480\t\n   150\t   blobs both carry it; only branches add :level/:addresses).\"\n   151\t  [x]\n   152\t  (and (map? x) (contains? x :keys)))\n   153\t\n   154\t\n   155\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   156\t;; Snapshot reading and read-back\n   157\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   158\t\n   159\t(deftest publish-index-snapshot-reads-local-stream-and-reads-back\n   160\t  (testing\n   161\t    \"publish-index! walks the agent's local stream from {:position 0}, and\n   162\t          the published manifest reads back the exact datoms through\n   163\t          read-manifest / read-datoms after observer materialization\"\n   164\t    (let [datoms (datoms 300)\n   165\t          local (open-local datoms)\n   166\t          intake (open-intake)\n   167\t          {:keys [manifest-address manifest]} (index/publish-index! local\n   168\t                                                                    [intake])\n   169\t          store (materialize-through-observer [intake])]\n   170\t      (is (= (count datoms) (:count manifest)))\n   171\t      (is (= 512 (:branching-factor manifest)))\n   172\t      (is (= manifest (index/read-manifest store manifest-address)))\n   173\t      (is (= (set datoms) (set (index/read-datoms store manifest-address)))\n   174\t          \"the EAVT walk returns exactly the snapshot datoms\"))))\n   175\t\n   176\t\n   177\t(deftest publish-index-flattens-atomic-transaction-records\n   178\t  (testing\n   179\t    \"one local-stream element may carry an atomic transaction whose\n   180\t            datoms are flattened before the covered indexes are built\"\n   181\t    (let [tx-datoms [[1 :person/name \"Ada\" 7 1] [1 :person/role :architect 7 1]]\n   182\t          local (open-local [{:dao.space/transaction {:t 7,\n   183\t                                                      :datoms tx-datoms}}])\n   184\t          intake (open-intake)\n   185\t          {:keys [manifest-address manifest]} (index/publish-index! local\n   186\t                                                                    [intake])\n   187\t          store (materialize-through-observer [intake])]\n   188\t      (is (= tx-datoms (index/snapshot-datoms local)))\n   189\t      (is (= 2 (:count manifest)))\n   190\t      (is (= (set tx-datoms)\n   191\t             (set (index/read-datoms store manifest-address))))))\n   192\t  (testing \"a malformed transaction packet fails before intake emission\"\n   193\t    (doseq [packet [{:dao.space/transaction {:t 1, :datoms :not-a-vector}}\n   194\t                    {:dao.space/transaction {:t 1, :datoms []}}\n   195\t                    {:dao.space/transaction {:t 1,\n   196\t                                             :datoms [[1 :test/a :v 2 1]]}}\n   197\t                    {:dao.space/transaction\n   198\t                     {:t 1, :datoms [[1 :test/a :v 1 1 :source/forbidden]]}}]]\n   199\t      (let [local (open-local [packet])\n   200\t            intake (open-intake)]\n   201\t        (is (thrown-with-msg? #?(:cljs js/Error\n   202\t                                 :cljd Object\n   203\t                                 :default Exception)\n   204\t                              #\"transaction\"\n   205\t              (index/publish-index! local [intake])))\n   206\t        (is (empty? (intake-values intake)))))))\n   207\t\n   208\t\n   209\t(deftest publish-index-rejects-noncanonical-local-datom-slots\n   210\t  (testing\n   211\t    \"persisted d5 datoms require integer entity/time/metadata coordinates and\n   212\t          a keyword attribute before any intake payload is emitted\"\n   213\t    (doseq [bad-datom [[:entity :test/a :v 0 1] [-16 :test/a :v 0 1]\n   214\t                       [1 :unqualified :v 0 1] [1 \"test/a\" :v 0 1]\n   215\t                       [1 :test/a :v -1 1] [1 :test/a :v 0 :db/assert]]]\n   216\t      (let [local (open-local [bad-datom])\n   217\t            intake (open-intake)]\n   218\t        (is (thrown-with-msg? #?(:cljs js/Error\n   219\t                                 :cljd Object\n   220\t                                 :default Exception)\n   221\t                              #\"datom\"\n   222\t              (index/publish-index! local [intake])))\n   223\t        (is (empty? (intake-values intake)))))))\n   224\t\n   225\t\n   226\t(deftest datoms-from-elements-is-the-local-stream-vocabulary\n   227\t  (testing\n   228\t    \"an element is one canonical d5 datom vector or one atomic transaction\n   229\t          record; the seq-level spelling flattens both, in stream order\"\n   230\t    (let [d1 [1 :person/name \"Ada\" 0 1]\n   231\t          d2 [1 :person/role :architect 7 1]\n   232\t          d3 [2 :work/status :todo 7 1]]\n   233\t      (is (= [] (index/datoms-from-elements [])))\n   234\t      (is (= [d1] (index/datoms-from-elements [d1])))\n   235\t      (is (= [d1 d2 d3]\n   236\t             (index/datoms-from-elements\n   237\t               [d1 {:dao.space/transaction {:t 7, :datoms [d2 d3]}}])))))\n   238\t  (testing \"a transaction record is exactly {:t n :datoms [canonical d5]}\"\n   239\t    (let [tx-datoms [[1 :person/name \"Ada\" 7 1] [1 :person/role :architect 7 1]]]\n   240\t      (is (= tx-datoms\n   241\t             (index/datoms-from-elements\n   242\t               [{:dao.space/transaction {:t 7, :datoms tx-datoms}}]))))\n   243\t    (doseq [packet [{:dao.space/transaction {:t 1, :datoms :not-a-vector}}\n   244\t                    {:dao.space/transaction {:t 1, :datoms []}}\n   245\t                    {:dao.space/transaction {:t 1,\n   246\t                                             :datoms [[1 :test/a :v 2 1]]}}\n   247\t                    {:dao.space/transaction\n   248\t                     {:t 1, :datoms [[1 :test/a :v 1 1 :source/forbidden]]}}\n   249\t                    {:dao.space/transaction {:t -1,\n   250\t                                             :datoms [[1 :test/a :v -1 1]]}}\n   251\t                    {:dao.space/transaction\n   252\t                     {:t 1, :datoms [[1 :test/a :v 1 1]], :extra :key}}]]\n   253\t      (is (thrown-with-msg? #?(:cljs js/Error\n   254\t                               :cljd Object\n   255\t                               :default Exception)\n   256\t                            #\"malformed dao\\.space transaction record\"\n   257\t            (index/datoms-from-elements [packet]))\n   258\t          (str \"must reject \" (pr-str packet)))))\n   259\t  (testing\n   260\t    \"a malformed element throws its distinct diagnostic — a malformed datom\n   261\t          is not a malformed transaction record, and a non-element is neither\"\n   262\t    (doseq [bad-datom [[:entity :test/a :v 0 1] [-16 :test/a :v 0 1]\n   263\t                       [1 :unqualified :v 0 1] [1 \"test/a\" :v 0 1]\n   264\t                       [1 :test/a :v -1 1] [1 :test/a :v 0 :db/assert]]]\n   265\t      (is (thrown-with-msg? #?(:cljs js/Error\n   266\t                               :cljd Object\n   267\t                               :default Exception)\n   268\t                            #\"malformed local datom\"\n   269\t            (index/datoms-from-elements [bad-datom]))\n   270\t          (str \"must reject datom \" (pr-str bad-datom))))\n   271\t    (doseq [bad [\"not-an-element\" [1 :test/a :v] {:not :a-transaction}]]\n   272\t      (is (thrown-with-msg? #?(:cljs js/Error\n   273\t                               :cljd Object\n   274\t                               :default Exception)\n   275\t                            #\"local stream payload must be\"\n   276\t            (index/datoms-from-elements [bad]))\n   277\t          (str \"must reject \" (pr-str bad)))))\n   278\t  (testing\n   279\t    \"the public spelling and the snapshot read agree on the same elements:\n   280\t          both go through the same per-element rule\"\n   281\t    (let [d1 [1 :person/name \"Ada\" 0 1]\n   282\t          d2 [1 :person/role :architect 7 1]\n   283\t          d3 [2 :work/status :todo 7 1]\n   284\t          elements [d1 {:dao.space/transaction {:t 7, :datoms [d2 d3]}}]]\n   285\t      (is (= (index/datoms-from-elements elements)\n   286\t             (index/snapshot-datoms (open-local elements)))))))\n   287\t\n   288\t\n   289\t(deftest publish-index-address-is-content-derived-and-stream-invariant\n   290\t  (testing\n   291\t    \"the manifest address derives from the manifest alone: identical local\n   292\t          data published through different intake streams and pools converges\n   293\t          on the same address\"\n   294\t    (let [datoms (datoms 200)\n   295\t          local-a (open-local datoms)\n   296\t          local-b (open-local datoms)\n   297\t          ra (index/publish-index! local-a [(open-intake) (open-intake)])\n   298\t          rb (index/publish-index! local-b [(open-intake) (open-intake)])]\n   299\t      (is (= (:manifest ra) (:manifest rb)))\n   300\t      (is (= (jing/segment-key (:manifest ra)) (:manifest-address ra)))\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:69:  vector `[e a v t m]` or one atomic transaction record\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:70:  `{:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms [...]}}`; transaction records are\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:73:  datoms, or transaction records throw. Because the snapshot starts at\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:161:`{:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms [...]}}` record; `datoms-from-elements`\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:164:transaction records, and non-elements with distinct diagnostics. It has no\n